Sine and cosine oscillate instead of growing. They are the same curve shifted by one quarter of a cycle, and differentiation rotates them into one another: $\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{dx}\sin(kx)&=k\cos(kx),\\
\frac{d}{dx}\cos(kx)&=-k\sin(kx).\end{aligned}$ Neither function alone is an exponential, because its derivative is proportional to the other function. The complex combination does have the exponential property. With $i^2=-1$, $\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{dx}\bigl(\cos(kx)+i\sin(kx)\bigr)
&=-k\sin(kx)+ik\cos(kx)\\
&=ik\bigl(\cos(kx)+i\sin(kx)\bigr).\end{aligned}$ Therefore $e^{ikx}=\cos(kx)+i\sin(kx).$ This identity comes up repeatedly because sines and cosines describe wave-like oscillations. When $x$ is a spatial coordinate, the function oscillates through space. When the argument is time, it oscillates in time.

